discarded W5 and meld those three into an exposed Chow, freeing space.)  \n- W7,W7 could be a pair.  \n- T6,B2 need companions: maybe T6 can form Chow with T4,T5 or T5,T7 etc, but T5 all gone (my kong used all 4 T5), so T6 is dead unless paired or pung. B2 similar.\n\nIf I do \"Chi W5\":  \nAfter melding [W4,W5,W6], hand: W7,W7,T6,B2.  \nRemaining: one pair (W7,W7), T6,B2 need two melds: almost impossible unless draw T6 or B2 for pungs, or draw sequences with them. Very risky.  \n\nIf I \"Chi W6\":  \nMeld [W5,W6,W7] using my W5,W7 and claimed W6, hand: W4,W7,T6,B2 (W7 left one, other W7 used in meld). Now hand messy.\n\nIf I **Pass**:  \nHand: W4,W5,W6,W7,W7,T6,B2.  \nI could try to self-draw W8 (1 left) or W3 (2 left) to make Chow W6,W7,W8 or W4,W5,W6 already possible without exposing. Keeping hand concealed may be better for scoring (Concealed Hand points). Also, by passing, I keep flexibility to possibly form two Chows from W4-W7 and a pair from T6 or B2 later.  \n\nGiven board state:  \n- Prevailing wind North (F4), Seat wind South (F2) \u2014 none in my hand now.  \n- Many melds exposed by others, reducing tile availability. But W7 still 3 left in wall (total 4, I have 2, one exposed in previous player\u2019s meld [W6,W7,W8]? Wait, previous player\u2019s melds include [W6,W7,W8], so one W7 exposed, so remaining W7 = 4 total - 1 in that meld - my 2 = 1 left. Yes, 1 W7 left. So W7 pair is not safe to complete unless draw last W7.  \n\nBetter to keep hand closed and aim for a simple win with All Simples (W4\u2013W8, T6, B2 are all simples) and maybe Half Flush if I get more Characters? I have Characters (W) and one T6 and B2 now \u2014 not Half Flush yet.  \n\nConsidering all: passing seems better because exposing a Chow now reduces hand flexibility for minimal gain, and I need more tiles anyway. Chi now commits me to an exposed hand and I still need two more melds, unlikely to complete soon.\n\n**Reason:**  \nKeeping hand concealed retains more options and potential scoring patterns (Concealed Hand, Self-Drawn, etc.), while using Chi now fixes one meld but leaves a very incomplete remaining hand with poor odds to complete quickly. Pass and wait for better draws to improve hand shape.\n\n**Answer:** Pass"}
