card one tile now to get to 14 tiles total (hand + melds).  \n\nSo my task is to discard one tile to shape my hand toward a winning hand.  \n\nMy hand is:  \nW4, W4, W5, W6, W7, W7, T6, B2.  \n\nMelds are: Kong T5 (so T5 is dead for my hand), and Chow W2-W3-W4.  \n\nW suit looks promising \u2014 I have W4, W5, W6, W7 in hand, and W2 W3 W4 in meld. So I have many W tiles. Potential for Full Flush (all W) is possible but would require getting rid of T6 and B2. Full Flush is worth 24 points.  \n\nBut my melds include T5 Kong (which is not W) \u2014 so Full Flush is impossible because Kong T5 breaks same-suit requirement.  \n\nAnother possibility: Half Flush (only W + honors) is possible because T5 Kong and W2-W3-W4 Chow are already fixed, but Half Flush requires only honors and one suit \u2014 T5 is not honors nor W, so Half Flush impossible.  \n\nPotential All Types? I already have W, T, B, and honors (none in hand currently, but could get one). With Kong T5 (T suit), Chow W2-W3-W4 (W suit), B2 in hand (B suit) \u2014 could get an honor pair to complete All Types (worth 6). But to win, I need 8+ points total.  \n\nCurrent patterns so far from my melds:  \n- Kong T5 gives Melded Kong (1 point), and possibly Pung of Terminals/Honors? T5 is not terminal. So only Melded Kong 1 point.  \n- Chow W2-W3-W4 is All Chows component? But All Chows needs 4 chows + pair \u2014 not yet. Short Straight possible if combined with another W chow.  \n\nLooking at hand:  \nGrouping W tiles: W4 W4 W5 W6 W7 W7 \u2192 possible chows:  \n- W4 W5 W6 (needs one W5 or W6) \u2014 I have one W5, one W6, but W4 I have two; W4 W5 W6 uses W4, W5, W6, I have W4, W5, W6 \u2192 can make Chow W4 W5 W6 if I keep them.  \n- W5 W6 W7 needs W5, W6, W7 \u2014 I have them, could make Chow.  \n- W7 pair possible.  \n\nBut I need four melds total \u2014 I already have two melds (Kong and Chow), so need two more melds from hand.  \n\nPossible plan: try to form two chows from W tiles and a pair from W7 or other.  \n\nCurrently T6 and B2 are isolated. Best discard is one of them to aim for W-only hand (but not Full Flush because of T5 Kong). Still, could aim for All Chows (worth 2) + some other patterns.  \n\nBut must reach 8+ points to win.  \n\nCheck scoring: if I make two more chows in W suit, with W7 pair, patterns possible:  \n- All Chows (2)  \n- Short Straight (1) if two chows consecutive numbers in same suit (e.g., W4 W5 W6 and W5 W6 W7? overlapping numbers not allowed for Short Straight? Actually Short Straight is 234+567 in same suit, not necessarily disjoint). I have W4-W5-W6 and W5-W6-W7 \u2014 that\u2019s overlapping. Short Straight is two chows successive in same suit: e.g., 234+345 not allowed? Actually rules say \u201ctwo chows in same suit with successive numbers\u201d \u2014 successive means 2nd starts where 1st ends, like 234+345? Possibly not. But W4 W5 W6 (456) and W5 W6 W7 (567) are not successive numbers (456 and 567 overlap). So maybe no Short Straight.  \n\nPotential Pure Double Chow if I make two identical chows in W suit? Not likely with current tiles.  \n\nAlso possible: Edge Wait or Closed Wait or Single Wait depending on final shape.  \n\nTotal fans may be low: All Chows (2), Melded Kong (1), maybe Self Drawn (1), maybe One Voided Suit (1) if I discard B2 and T6. That\u2019s only 5 points \u2014 not enough to win.  \n\nI need at least 8. So must aim for higher-point patterns.  \n\nFrom current hand, maybe try for All Pungs (6) + Dragon Pung (2) etc.? But I have no pungs in hand.  \n\nAnother idea: Mixed Shifted Chows? Not likely.  \n\nGiven constraints, maybe better to keep hand flexible and discard the isolated tile B2 or T6.  \n\nWhich is safer? Check unshown tiles: B2 left 2, T6 left 2, W7 left 3. Discarding B2: only 2 left in wall, but opponents may need it. T6 left 2. Unshown: T7:0, T5:0 (my Kong used all). So T6 is somewhat safe since T5 gone, T7 gone, and T6-T7-T8 chows less likely for opponents.  \n\nBut opponent\u2019s melds: Next player has B8 pung, T6 T7 T8 chow, B4 B5 B6 chow \u2014 so they already used T6 in a chow, so they won\u2019t need another T6 much.  \n\nPrevious player melds: B1 pung, W6 W7 W8 chow, B5 B6 B7 chow, B2 B3 B4 chow \u2014 they have B2 in a chow, so maybe safe to discard B2? B2 left 2, they might need a pair?  \n\nGiven everything, discard B2 seems safe.  \n\nBut maybe T6 is safer because T5 and T7 are exhausted (T5:0, T7:0), so T6 can\u2019t form chows with T5/T7.  \n\nAlso T6 is terminal in the 6-7-8 chow? Actually T6 is middle, but T7 is gone, so no T6-T7-T8 chow possible for others (next player already has one, but that\u2019s only one of four copies).  \n\nStill, discard T6 may be slightly safer.  \n\nGoal: to eventually form winning hand, need to draw W tiles. Discard B2 first.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nB2 is isolated with no connection to other tiles in my hand (only B suit, one tile). T6 connects weakly to W? No, but T6 could be kept for a mixed chow if I draw T5/T7 (impossible, T5 gone, T7 gone), so T6 is dead. B2 could pair if I draw another B2 (2 left), but that doesn\u2019t help winning quickly. Better to discard B2 to reduce suit diversity and aim for W-based hand, hoping to draw W8, W3, W1 etc., to complete chows. T6 also discardable, but B2 is slightly more disconnected from potential patterns.  \n\nI will discard B2 now to keep W tiles for possible chows.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B2"}
